-- kopperman.mm0 — the Kopperman Hilbert-space layer of the logos theory.
--
-- This file is an *overlay*: the emitter concatenates `logos_math.mm0` (the
-- agent/Peano layer) with this file to form the full target theory of a
-- Hilbert-space claim (`KTHEORY` in `src/mm0/emit.rs`). It is written as an
-- overlay so the agent layer has exactly one copy: both fragments share
-- `Prop`, `Term`, `teq`, the numerals and the bridge, and a certificate for
-- either fragment is one self-contained `.mm0`/`.mm1` pair.
--
-- The prover this grounds is based on Kopperman's Hilbert-space axioms:
-- R. Kopperman, "The L_{ω1,ω1}-Theory of Hilbert Spaces" (1967), as packaged
-- by `Kopperman_Tutorial.p.tex` and `BookProof/Toolbox/PhysMehler.lean` in
-- the sibling `timepiece` repo (the `Formalism` structure). Its three
-- layers match those sources:
--
--   K1. The real system — the scalar field as Kopperman's "reflexive real
--       system": an ordered field with square/cube roots (real closed — the
--       Solovay/Tarski scalar theory of `timepiece/Book`, whose decidable
--       core is ℝ_alg, the algebraic reals) and Dedekind completeness in
--       ε-form. The Peano numerals `tz`/`ts` inside `Term` are the
--       *computable skeleton* — the decidable dense subset — not the whole
--       field: `tsqrt (ts tz)` exists and is not a numeral, which is the
--       honest shape of a completed real system over a decidable core.
--
--   K2. The Hilbert system — finite-head vectors and their inner product.
--       A vector is its head, `tvnil`/`tvcons` (`InnerHead N = Fin N → ℝ`
--       of `timepiece/RandomMap/RandomMap2.lean`, spelled as a list); the
--       recursion rules make the decidable fragment *compute* (they are the
--       rewrite system `src/mm0/kop.rs` normalises with), and the laws
--       below are the inner-product axioms themselves. Positive
--       definiteness uses hypothesis-binder form like `logos_math.mm0`:
--       `> ` is the applicable inference, never `->` inside a formula.
--
--   K3. The ontological layer — sequences, limits, the Riesz–Fischer
--       skeleton of completeness, and the decidable dense subset. No CNL
--       sentence can name a limit: Kopperman's Warning (Section V, 1967) is
--       that one named infinite constant destroys decidability, and the
--       fragment honours it — `tlimit`/`tsup` appear only in theory axioms,
--       never in a certificate this prover builds.
--
-- Standard model (what makes the axioms sound): scalars in the complete
-- real closed field ℝ; a vector term denotes its coordinate function
-- ℕ → ℝ (finite support); `teq` is equality of denotations; junk
-- applications of operations outside their intended arguments are total and
-- unconstrained. Every axiom below holds under that interpretation — the
-- same style of consistency claim `logos_math.mm0` makes for its layer.
--
-- The sequence-layer predicates (`tcauchy`, `tsummable`, `tconv`,
-- `tbounded`) are primitives constrained by K1/K3's axioms: mm0's term
-- language is first-order, so Kopperman's L_{ω1,ω1} sequence quantifiers
-- are reified as terms with `tapply` — exactly the move the tutorial itself
-- makes in UPL ("we model these infinite quantifiers via dependent types").
-- The ε-form statements they *can* carry (Dedekind completeness, the
-- Riesz–Fischer shape of `BookProof/ChapterRieszFischer`) are stated in
-- full; the remaining ε–δ content is noted where it belongs, not faked.

-- ── K1. The real system ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- Order. Primitive predicates; the connectives live in hypothesis binders.
term tle (a b: Term): Prop;
term tlt (a b: Term): Prop;

axiom le_refl (a: Term): $ tle a a $;
axiom le_antisym (a b: Term): $ tle a b $ > $ tle b a $ > $ teq a b $;
axiom le_trans (a b c: Term): $ tle a b $ > $ tle b c $ > $ tle a c $;
axiom lt_le (a b: Term): $ tlt a b $ > $ tle a b $;
axiom lt_irrefl (a: Term): $ tlt a a $ > $ tfals $;
axiom lt_asym (a b: Term): $ tlt a b $ > $ tlt b a $ > $ tfals $;
axiom le_add_r (a b c: Term): $ tle a b $ > $ tle (tadd a c) (tadd b c) $;
axiom le_mul_nonneg (a b: Term): $ tle tz a $ > $ tle tz b $ > $ tle tz (tmul a b) $;

-- Field laws of the scalar operations: `tadd`/`tmul` read as the field
-- operations (the standard model has `ts a = a + 1`), consistent with the
-- Peano recursion of `logos_math.mm0`, which also holds in ℝ.
axiom add_comm (a b: Term): $ teq (tadd a b) (tadd b a) $;
axiom add_assoc (a b c: Term): $ teq (tadd (tadd a b) c) (tadd a (tadd b c)) $;
axiom mul_comm (a b: Term): $ teq (tmul a b) (tmul b a) $;
axiom mul_assoc (a b c: Term): $ teq (tmul (tmul a b) c) (tmul a (tmul b c)) $;
axiom distrib (a b c: Term): $ teq (tmul a (tadd b c)) (tadd (tmul a b) (tmul a c)) $;

-- Real closed field: every positive element has a square root, every
-- element a cube root. Full RCF is the schema over all odd degrees; degrees
-- 1 and 3 are the instances statable without degree-indexed symbols, and
-- this is recorded rather than glossed (the axiom is what the fragment
-- uses: `sqrt` of a positive numeral exists, and is not a numeral).
term tsqrt (a: Term): Term;
term tcbrt (a: Term): Term;
axiom rcf_sqrt (a: Term): $ tlt tz a $ > $ teq (tmul (tsqrt a) (tsqrt a)) a $;
axiom rcf_cbrt (a: Term): $ teq (tmul (tmul (tcbrt a) (tcbrt a)) (tcbrt a)) a $;

-- Dedekind completeness, in ε-form: a bounded scalar sequence has a least
-- upper bound, approached within e > 0. `s` ranges over sequence terms
-- (K3); `tapply s n` is the n-th element.
term tapply (s n: Term): Term;
term tbounded (s M: Term): Prop;
term tsup (s: Term): Term;
term tnear (s e: Term): Term;
axiom sup_upper (s M: Term): $ tbounded s M $ > $ tle (tsup s) M $;
axiom sup_approx (s M e: Term):
  $ tbounded s M $ > $ tlt tz e $ > $ tle (tapply s (tnear s e)) (tadd (tsup s) e) $;

-- ── K2. The Hilbert system: finite-head vectors ─────────────────────────────

term tvnil: Term;
term tvcons (c v: Term): Term;
term tcoord (v i: Term): Term;
term ttail (v: Term): Term;
term tvadd (u v: Term): Term;
term tvscal (s v: Term): Term;
term tinpr (u v: Term): Term;

-- Coordinates: `tcoord` reads position `i`, `tvcons` puts `c` at 0 and
-- shifts the tail right. These four rules are what makes a head *compute*.
axiom coord_nil (i: Term): $ teq (tcoord tvnil i) tz $;
axiom coord_cons_z (c v: Term): $ teq (tcoord (tvcons c v) tz) c $;
axiom coord_cons_s (c v i: Term): $ teq (tcoord (tvcons c v) (ts i)) (tcoord v i) $;
axiom tail_nil: $ teq (ttail tvnil) tvnil $;
axiom tail_cons (c v: Term): $ teq (ttail (tvcons c v)) v $;

-- Vector addition: coordinatewise, `tvnil` padded from either side (the
-- zero vector is the identity of every head — both rules are needed, and
-- together they are confluent: nil/nil meets only `vadd_nil`).
axiom vadd_nil (v: Term): $ teq (tvadd tvnil v) v $;
axiom vadd_cons_nil (c v: Term): $ teq (tvadd (tvcons c v) tvnil) (tvcons c v) $;
axiom vadd_cons_cons (c d u v: Term):
  $ teq (tvadd (tvcons c u) (tvcons d v)) (tvcons (tadd c d) (tvadd u v)) $;

-- Scalar multiplication.
axiom vscal_nil (s: Term): $ teq (tvscal s tvnil) tvnil $;
axiom vscal_cons (s c v: Term):
  $ teq (tvscal s (tvcons c v)) (tvcons (tmul s c) (tvscal s v)) $;

-- The inner product: recursion on the first head, reading the second's
-- coordinates. For literal heads this computes through the Peano layer
-- (that is the decidable Kopperman–Solovay fragment); the laws below say
-- what it is in general.
axiom inpr_nil (v: Term): $ teq (tinpr tvnil v) tz $;
axiom inpr_cons (c u v: Term):
  $ teq (tinpr (tvcons c u) v)
        (tadd (tmul c (tcoord v tz)) (tinpr u (ttail v))) $;

-- The inner-product laws (Kopperman's Hilbert system). Symmetry and
-- bilinearity hold in the standard model (the recursion above is the exact
-- Σ over both supports) but are not *derivable* from the recursion without
-- induction — so they are axioms, exactly as in Kopperman's system, and a
-- claim like "the inner product of u and v equals that of v and u" closes
-- by citing `inpr_sym`, replayed by mm0.
axiom inpr_sym (u v: Term): $ teq (tinpr u v) (tinpr v u) $;
axiom inpr_add_l (u v w: Term):
  $ teq (tinpr (tvadd u v) w) (tadd (tinpr u w) (tinpr v w)) $;
axiom inpr_add_r (u v w: Term):
  $ teq (tinpr u (tvadd v w)) (tadd (tinpr u v) (tinpr u w)) $;
axiom inpr_scal_l (s u v: Term):
  $ teq (tinpr (tvscal s u) v) (tmul s (tinpr u v)) $;
axiom inpr_scal_r (s u v: Term):
  $ teq (tinpr u (tvscal s v)) (tmul s (tinpr u v)) $;
-- Positive definiteness: ‖v‖² = 0 implies v is the zero vector.
axiom inpr_pos (v: Term): $ teq (tinpr v v) tz $ > $ teq v tvnil $;

-- Congruence: to rewrite inside a vector term each constructor transports
-- the equality of its arguments (the `eq_app` of the agent layer, per
-- constructor). Without these the normaliser could only rewrite at the root.
axiom eq_tvcons (a b u v: Term): $ teq a b $ > $ teq u v $ > $ teq (tvcons a u) (tvcons b v) $;
axiom eq_tcoord (u v i j: Term): $ teq u v $ > $ teq i j $ > $ teq (tcoord u i) (tcoord v j) $;
axiom eq_ttail (u v: Term): $ teq u v $ > $ teq (ttail u) (ttail v) $;
axiom eq_tvadd (a b c d: Term): $ teq a b $ > $ teq c d $ > $ teq (tvadd a c) (tvadd b d) $;
axiom eq_tvscal (s t u v: Term): $ teq s t $ > $ teq u v $ > $ teq (tvscal s u) (tvscal t v) $;
axiom eq_tinpr (a b c d: Term): $ teq a b $ > $ teq c d $ > $ teq (tinpr a c) (tinpr b d) $;
axiom eq_tapply (s t n m: Term): $ teq s t $ > $ teq n m $ > $ teq (tapply s n) (tapply t m) $;

-- ── K3. The ontological layer: sequences, limits, density ───────────────────
--
-- Kopperman's L_{ω1,ω1} quantifies over sequences; in a first-order term
-- language a sequence is reified as a term with `tapply` (the tutorial's
-- own note: "we model these infinite quantifiers via dependent types").
-- The predicates are primitive and constrained by the completeness and
-- density axioms below; no CNL sentence can name a limit — Kopperman's
-- Warning (Section V, 1967): one named infinite constant destroys
-- decidability, by way of isolating one number, reconstructing the
-- integers, and importing undecidable Peano Arithmetic.

term tcauchy (s: Term): Prop;
term tlimit (s: Term): Term;
term tconv (s: Term): Prop;
axiom cauchy_complete (s: Term): $ tcauchy s $ > $ tconv s $;
axiom conv_limit (s: Term): $ tconv s $ > $ teq (tlimit s) (tlimit s) $;

-- Riesz–Fischer skeleton: a summable series of vectors has a sum, and the
-- sum is the limit of its partial sums — the shape of Book's verified core
-- (`completeSpace_iff_summable_norm`, `BookProof/ChapterRieszFischer`).
term tsummable (s: Term): Prop;
term tpartial (s: Term): Term;
term tseries (s: Term): Term;
axiom riesz_fischer (s: Term): $ tsummable s $ > $ teq (tseries s) (tlimit (tpartial s)) $;

-- The decidable dense subset: every vector is approximated, within e > 0,
-- by a rational-coefficient finite head — the computable skeleton of
-- `PhysMehler.Formalism` (`skeleton_countable` + `skeleton_dense`), with
-- the existential packaged as a witness term because mm0 formulas carry
-- binders, not quantifiers over `Term`.
term trat (v: Term): Prop;
term tclose (u v e: Term): Prop;
term tvapprox (v e: Term): Term;
axiom rat_approx (v e: Term):
  $ tlt tz e $ > $ trat (tvapprox v e) $ > $ tclose (tvapprox v e) v e $;
